<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/html-entities.dtd">

<topic 
	xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" 
	xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
	title="Organizations" id="cloud-organizations">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="3"/>

    <link-summary>Organizations provide top-level structuring of all entities in Qodana Cloud. Every organization is
        created on the basis of a specific JetBrains account.</link-summary>

    <p>Organizations provide top-level structuring of all entities in <a href="https://qodana.cloud">Qodana Cloud</a>.
    Every organization is created on the basis of a specific JetBrains account. Depending on your choice, it will be either
        private or public.
        Every account in Qodana Cloud is based on the organization that becomes its default organization.
    </p>

    <p>You can navigate between organizations using the list in the upper left part of the Qodana Cloud UI.</p>

    <img src="qc-settings-organization-navigate-between.animated.gif" dark-src="qc-settings-organization-navigate-between_dark.png" width="706" alt="Creating an organization" border-effect="line" preview-src="qc-settings-organization-navigate-between.png"/>

    <chapter id="cloud-organizations-create-organization" title="Create an organization">

        <p>You can create private or public organizations. Reports stored in private organizations are accessible only
            by a user who created an organization and users who were invited to them.
            Reports stored in public organizations are available for all Qodana Cloud users, including unauthorized users with the
            <a href="cloud-user-roles.md">viewer role</a>. </p>

        <p>Each organization is created on the basis of a specific <a href="cloud-get-access.topic">JetBrains account</a>.</p>

        <procedure>
            <step>
                <p>In the sidebar, expand the list of organizations and then click
                    <ui-path>Create organization</ui-path>.</p>

                <img src="qc-settings-organization-create.png" dark-src="qc-settings-organization-create_dark.png" width="706" alt="Creating an organization" border-effect="line"/>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>Complete the project setup stage. You can read more about this stage in the <a href="set-up-your-project.md"/> section.</p>
            </step>
        </procedure>
    </chapter>

    <chapter id="cloud-organizations-overview" title="Configure organizations">

        <p>You can configure membership and visibility of your organization, or leave the organization.</p>

        <procedure>

            <step>
                <p>In the sidebar, navigate to the organization that you wish to configure. </p>
                <img src="qc-settings-organization-navigate-between.animated.gif" dark-src="qc-settings-organization-navigate-between_dark.png" width="706" alt="Creating an organization" border-effect="line" preview-src="qc-settings-organization-navigate-between.png"/>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>On the organization page, click the <ui-path>Organization settings</ui-path> button in the upper-right corner.</p>
                <img src="qc-settings-organization-manage-access.png" dark-src="qc-settings-organization-manage-access_dark.png" width="706" alt="Navigate to the organization settings" border-effect="line"/>
            </step>

        </procedure>

        <p>On the settings page, you can find the following tabs:</p>

        <list>
            <li><ui-path>Subscription</ui-path> contains the %instance% license information.</li>
            <li><ui-path>Logs</ui-path> contains the list of log entries associated with your organization.</li>
            <li><ui-path>Members</ui-path> contains the list of organization members. Using this tab, you can
                <a anchor="cloud-organizations-invitation">manage your organization membership</a>. </li>
            <li><ui-path>Global configurations</ui-path> let you manage <a href="global-configuration.md">configurations used across many projects</a> of your organization.</li>
        </list>

        <p>Click the button in the upper-right corner of the UI to switch the status of your organization, rename it, leave it, or completely delete it.</p>

        <img src="qc-settings-organization-leave.png" dark-src="qc-settings-organization-leave_dark.png" width="706" alt="Leaving and switching organization status" border-effect="line"/>

        <note>You cannot leave or delete an organization in case you are the only member of it, so you need to assign a new
            administrator beforehand.</note>

        <chapter id="cloud-organizations-invitation" title="Organization membership">

            <p>Within your organization, you can:</p>

            <list>
                <li>Invite new members using email addresses, and assign them <a href="cloud-user-roles.md">user roles</a>.</li>
                <li>Change <a href="cloud-user-roles.md">the roles</a> of the existing organization members.</li>
                <li>Exclude members from your organization.</li>
            </list>

            <procedure>
                <step>
                    <p>On the <a anchor="cloud-organizations-overview">settings page</a> of the organization, navigate
                        to the <ui-path>Members</ui-path> tab.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>On the <ui-path>Members</ui-path> tab, click <ui-path>Invite members</ui-path>.</p>
                    <img src="qc-settings-organization-invite-members.png" dark-src="qc-settings-organization-invite-members_dark.png" width="706" alt="Clicking the Invite members button" border-effect="line"/>

                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>On the <ui-path>Invite members to ... organization</ui-path> dialog that opens, you can manage members of your organization.</p>
                    <img src="qc-settings-organization-manage-members.png" dark-src="qc-settings-organization-manage-members.png" width="706" alt="Managing organization membership" border-effect="line"/>
                </step>
            </procedure>

        </chapter>

        <chapter id="cloud-organizations-global-configurations" title="Global configurations">
            <p>Using this tab, you can manage <a href="global-configuration.md">global configurations</a>. On this tab,
                you can find the list of configurations that can be used across multiple projects of your organization.
            Here, you can view the list of available configurations, number of projects connected to a global configuration,
            configuration description and the time of the latest configuration update. You can also navigate to the VCS repository
            containing the global configuration, as well as copy and revoke a token.</p>

            <p>Using the list of projects, you can view the projects connected to the configuration, whether this configuration
            was used for analysis, search for specific projects, see if there are projects with
                <a href="global-configuration.md" anchor="Global+and+local+configurations">global and local configurations</a>,
            see only projects that have local and global configuration conflicts, dive into conflicts, and view and assign
                global configurations to specific projects.</p>
            <p>Here, you can also modify the list of projects that use the specific global configuration.</p>

            <chapter id="cloud-organizations-global-configurations-connect-a-repository" title="Connect a repository">
                <procedure>
                    <step>
                        <p>On the <ui-path>Global configurations</ui-path> tab, click the <ui-path>Connect repository</ui-path> button.</p>
                    </step>
                    <step>On the dialog that opens, configure your CI/CD solution.</step>
                    <!-- On the second step, the meaning of the token should be explained to the user, and that this is by default is hidden -->
                </procedure>

                <p>Once the first run of a CI/CD solution with the uploader is complete, the global configuration is uploaded
                and becomes visible on the <ui-path>Global configurations</ui-path> tab.</p>

                <!-- The image of the Global Configurations tab should be provided here -->

                <chapter id="cloud-organizations-global-configurations-modify-global-configuration" title="Modify a global configuration">
                    <p>Click the configuration that you would like to modify. This will open the dialog containing all
                        configuration parameters. On this dialog, you can view the files contained in the global configuration
                        and obtain a link to the VCS to be able to edit its files. The lower part of the dialog contains a configuration. </p>
                    <!-- The image is required here -->
                </chapter>

                <chapter id="cloud-organizations-global-configurations-view-modify-list-of-projects" title="View and modify the list of projects">
                    <p>Click the number of projects sharing a specific configuration to open a dialog containing the list
                        of projects. Besides that, you can also see information about the latest
                    inspection analysis performed over a specific project. Here, you can view, assign and unassign a global
                    configuration to a specific project or projects. Both local and global parts of the configuration
                    are shown here with each configuration highlighted.</p>
                    <!-- Image of projects is required here -->
                </chapter>

            </chapter>

            <chapter id="cloud-organizations-global-configurations-revoke-regenerate-token" title="Revoke and regenerate the token">
                <p>In case a token is compromised, regenerate the token and clear all configurations
                    (because an attacker could’ve used the token and uploaded their malicious configuration).
                    The user story here is token was available not to malicious actor, but we need to regenerate it.
                    For example, this can be useful if an administrator has left the company.</p>
            </chapter>
            <chapter title="Update the configuration">
                <p>On this page, you can find the tabs containing files related to the global configuration. </p>
            </chapter>
        </chapter>

    </chapter>



</topic>
